## Setting up Portainer on the Pi

I got the Pi 5 running Docker Engine, no GUI, just a command line over SSH. Vaultwarden is up and running as my password manager; Caddy is handling the reverse proxy. But every time I want to check if something's actually running or see why a container looks kind of off, I'm typing "docker ps" and then looking for the terminal output that wraps weirdly on my screen, and it's a process. 
Now, I know in a real work setting I'm gonna have to look at the terminal a lot, which is completely fine, but what if I can find something that resembles Docker Desktop. 

I cannot use  Docker Desktop, which creates a dashboard for everything, because I have a Raspberry Pi OS Lite.
I was able to find a free tool called Portainer, which runs as its own container and gives you a web dashboard for Docker. I would have to open it in my own browser, and then view all of the containers I have, pull up logs, check the CPU and memory, and hit start, stop, restart, or console buttons instead of typing commands over SSH.

Portainer started in 2016 because Neil Cresswell was running a cloud hosting business in Asia and needed a management UI for Docker that just didn't exist yet, so he built one himself. He open-sourced it, and by New Year's it hit 10 million downloads. That's when he shut down the original cloud business and turned Portainer into an actual company founded in 2017.

*Credits: https://www.helpnetsecurity.com/2024/07/01/portainer-open-source-docker-kubernetes-management/

# Steps Taken

1. I need to 1st create a volume so Portainer's own data survives if the container gets rebuilt. On the command line, I type in: docker volume create portainer_data

Then run the Container itself: 
docker run -d \
  --name portainer \
  --restart unless-stopped \
  -p 9443:9443 \
  -v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock \
  -v portainer_data:/data \
  portainer/portainer-ce:latest

The image tag is just portainer-ce: latest; it's multi-arch, so Docker grabs the right version for the Pi 5 on its own.

"-v /var/run/docker.sock:/var/run/docker.sock. This mounts the host's Docker socket into the container; it lets Portainer see and control every other container on the box. Basically, Portainer isn't sandboxed off to the side; it's got the keys to Docker itself. Same kind of trust boundary as Vaultwarden's admin token, so the login on this thing needs to be treated just as seriously.

Port 9443 was just whatever was free. Pi-hole already had 80, Caddy had 443, so 9443 didn't step on anything.



2. I figured I'd hit the login page and just get asked to set a username and password, like most self-hosted things. Instead, I got a screen asking for a setup token, which I didn't have and wasn't expecting. I researched how to use the following token to log in and found the command. I typed in "docker logs portainer". Small catch: the token expires within about 5 minutes, so I needed to work fast. 


